<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_section" style="--ltx-fg-color:#2E5AA8;">1  Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Linear-attention language models replace the key–value cache of a Transformer with a recurrent
state of fixed size and have become competitive with Transformers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>.
Every recurrent layer holds one state matrix per head, and a server stores one copy for every
sequence it decodes at the same time. At 64 concurrent sequences, the fp32 states take between 10
and 41 % of the memory that weights and states occupy together. Storing the state at lower
precision lets more sequences fit on the same accelerator.
Existing state quantizers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>
differ in how they scale and round the state, and all of them round it after every generated token.
With per-token rounding, the methods that evaluate a four-bit state report a loss in accuracy (Figure <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). The published remedies either
raise the bit width to eight bits or fine-tune part of the model weights. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">NVIDIA et al. [20]</a></cite> round the state less often, but only with eight or more bits per value.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">During prefill, chunked kernels process <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>64</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1{,}024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1,024</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1{,}024</annotation></semantics></math> tokens at a time
and write the state only at the end of each chunk, so every published quantizer already rounds the
prefill state once per chunk. During decoding the kernel is called once per generated token, so the
state is written, and rounded, after every token (Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>a).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we introduce PACE (Periodic And Channel-aligned state Encoding), a post-hoc four-bit
state quantizer that requires no training (Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). During decoding, PACE
rounds the state once every <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dP" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dP</annotation></semantics></math> generated tokens (the decode period) instead of after every token (\scriptsizeA⃝). The tokens
generated in between are held in a decode buffer, a small store of recent keys and values from which
the state is advanced in one step. Recent serving work uses such buffers to speed up decoding
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. The buffer occupies memory, and
every memory total we report counts it together with the state (\scriptsizeB⃝).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The gated-delta state has one row per key dimension and one column per channel of the delta value,
the vector the recurrence writes into the state at each token. Channels that stay large across many
tokens produce columns of large entries, and when the channels are permuted at every token, the large
entries no longer form columns (Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Channel Structure of the Recurrent State ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). PACE therefore forms each scale group
from the entries of one column, that is, along the key axis (\scriptsizeC⃝). PACE has two variants:
PACE-4 keeps all heads at four bits, and PACE-4/8 keeps the quarter of heads in each layer that retain
rounding error longest at eight bits (\scriptsizeD⃝).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Our contributions are:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A bound on the rounding error a head retains as a function of the rounding period and the
head’s decay (Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Rounding Period and Error Propagation ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), together with a memory accounting that includes the decode
buffer (Section <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Periodic Rounding with a Decode Buffer (\scriptsizeA⃝\scriptsizeB⃝) ‣ 5 Method ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>). On four backbones, rounding every eight generated tokens
reduces the accuracy loss at every bit width below eight (Section <a href="#S7.SS1" title="7.1 Effect of the Decode Period ‣ 7 Results ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The observation that the large entries of the gated-delta state form columns, explained by
channels of the delta value that stay large across tokens and confirmed by permuting those channels (Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Channel Structure of the Recurrent State ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">PACE, a four-bit state quantizer with channel-aligned scale groups and head-level precision
allocation (Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Method ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), compared with DAMP and Q-Mamba on four hybrid models from 4B to
35B parameters (Section <a href="#S7.SS2" title="7.2 Main Results ‣ 7 Results ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.2</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Triton kernels for periodic decoding with a packed four-bit state, with which we show that
rounding every few tokens keeps the decoding speedup of the decode buffer (Section <a href="#S7.SS5" title="7.5 Throughput ‣ 7 Results ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.5</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.29545v1/method_overview_letters.svg" id="S1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/247;" width="476" height="247"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Overview of PACE. (a) Prior state quantizers round the recurrent state after every generated
token; PACE rounds it once every <math id="S1.F2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dP" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dP</annotation></semantics></math> tokens (\scriptsizeA⃝), as prefill already does at each kernel chunk
boundary. (b) At each flush, the packed state is dequantized, advanced over the tokens held in the
decode buffer (\scriptsizeB⃝), and requantized in channel-aligned scale groups (\scriptsizeC⃝). PACE-4/8 also holds a quarter of the heads
in each layer at eight bits, selected once per checkpoint (\scriptsizeD⃝).</figcaption>
</figure>
