</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#2E5AA8;">2.3  Decode Buffers</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Decoding calls the kernel once per token, so the state is written every step and the chunk structure is
lost. Recent serving work recovers it with a decode buffer. Recent keys and values are held, and the state is
advanced once per flush, the step at which the buffered tokens are applied, instead of once per token.
This removes the per-token write of the state from memory traffic.
KVBuffer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> derives an optimal buffer of about <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="23" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>23</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">23</annotation></semantics></math> tokens and reports its best
latency at <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>32</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32</annotation></semantics></math>, ReplaySSM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> uses <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16</annotation></semantics></math> for Qwen3.5, and DeltaLog
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> merges every <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math> tokens for Gated DeltaNet. None of them quantizes the state.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#2E5AA8;">2.4  State Quantization</h3>

<section id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Quantizing the recurrent state.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Quamba2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> stores cached states at eight bits with static scales. Q-Mamba
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> reaches four bits by scaling the state with one vector per axis and fine-tuning
about 2% of the weights. MambaQuant <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> rotates weights and activations before
rounding and leaves the state unquantized. DAMP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> ranks key channels by quantization error times decay persistence, keeps the riskiest in fp16 and reports <math id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9.9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9.9</annotation></semantics></math> bits per
value; it rounds once per chunk in prefill and after every token in decoding. The Nemotron report
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> keeps the state in fp16 with stochastic rounding, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">NVIDIA et al. [20]</a></cite>
store the state every eight decoding steps at eight bits and above, in emulation. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kozyrev and Maiboroda [13]</a></cite>
quantize the weights and activations of the recurrent layers and leave the state unquantized.
None of them varies the rounding period below eight bits or counts the buffer it requires.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Error propagation.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Rounding a recurrence is the fixed-point filter problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, in which rounding
noise of variance <math id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, injected at every step of a recurrence with decay <math id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>, accumulates to
<math id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}/(1-a^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}/(1-a^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. [33]</a></cite> rederive the
accumulated error for the gated delta rule and reduce the error per rounding; we reduce the number of
roundings (Eq. <a href="#S3.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ 3.2 Error Propagation ‣ 3 Rounding Period and Error Propagation ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kozyrev and Maiboroda [13]</a></cite> find that whole-state error stays flat from token <math id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>256</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256</annotation></semantics></math> on. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al. [34]</a></cite> clip the state during quantization-aware training and
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Siegel et al. [27]</a></cite> quantize an S4D state through its convolutional form.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">KV-cache quantization.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">KIVI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> and KVQuant <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> reach two to four bits on a cache whose
entries are written once and only read, and rotation methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> redistribute magnitude before rounding. A recurrent state is instead re-rounded at
every write.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_section" style="--ltx-fg-color:#2E5AA8;">3  Rounding Period and Error Propagation</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#2E5AA8;">3.1  The Rounding Period</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A quantizer can round the state only where the kernel writes it, which gives three regimes under Eq. <a href="#S2.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ 2.2 Chunk-wise Kernels ‣ 2 Background and Related Work ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>
(Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>a). In prefill the chunked kernel forms <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> as the initial state and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, so the state is rounded once per chunk without an additional kernel call. In unbuffered decoding each call carries one token,
<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is formed at every step, and the period is one. Under a decode buffer the state is advanced once per flush, and the period is the number of tokens the buffer holds.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We call one choice of bit width, scale groups, buffer precision and rounding periods for the
quantized state a <em id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">configuration</em>. Each configuration carries two periods. We write <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> for the prefill period, which equals the kernel chunk
length <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and which we fix at <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>256</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256</annotation></semantics></math> tokens in all benchmark evaluations, and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dP" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dP</annotation></semantics></math> for the decode period, which is the variable under study.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#2E5AA8;">3.2  Error Propagation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each rounding adds an error to the state, and the recurrence carries it forward. Between two
roundings the error is multiplied at every token by <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}(I-\beta_{t}k_{t}k_{t}^{\top})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}(I-\beta_{t}k_{t}k_{t}^{\top})</annotation></semantics></math>
(Eq. <a href="#S2.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 2.1 Gated Delta Rule ‣ 2 Background and Related Work ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), whose norm is at most <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> for a unit-norm key. With <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> the mean decay of head
<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>, an error of energy <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> injected at one rounding retains at most <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{h}^{2\,dP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{h}^{2\,dP}</annotation></semantics></math> of its energy at
the next, and independent errors from successive roundings accumulate to at most</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{h}=\frac{e_{h}}{1-a_{h}^{2\,dP}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{h}=\frac{e_{h}}{1-a_{h}^{2\,dP}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">A longer period lowers <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>, because more decay separates two roundings. In the gated delta rule the decay is one scalar per head, so
<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> is a per-head quantity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Finding 1.</span> The rounding error a head retains falls with the rounding period and
rises with the head’s decay</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_section" style="--ltx-fg-color:#2E5AA8;">4  Channel Structure of the Recurrent State</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A group-wise quantizer divides the state of each head into scale groups of <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> consecutive entries
and rounds all entries of a group with one shared step size, which is proportional to the largest
absolute entry of the group. A group runs either along the key axis, within one column of the state,
or along the value axis, within one row. When a group contains one large entry, its small entries are
rounded with a coarse step, so the error at four bits depends on which entries share a group.
Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Column Structure of the State ‣ 4 Channel Structure of the Recurrent State ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> measures along which axis the large entries of the state cluster,
and Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Channel Identity of the Delta Value ‣ 4 Channel Structure of the Recurrent State ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> traces this structure to the delta value.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#2E5AA8;">4.1  Column Structure of the State</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compute the state over <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2{,}048" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2,048</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2{,}048</annotation></semantics></math>-token WikiText-103 sequences on all four backbones. In a representative head the large entries form columns, that is, they occur at the same value index for every key index (Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2.2 Chunk-wise Kernels ‣ 2 Background and Related Work ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>a). We quantify this with the ratio of the largest absolute entry of a group to the root mean square of its entries (max/RMS). The larger the ratio, the fewer quantization levels a typical entry of the group receives. Groups along
the value axis, which cross columns, have the larger ratio at every group width, and the difference
grows with the width (Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2.2 Chunk-wise Kernels ‣ 2 Background and Related Work ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>b).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Finding 2.</span> The large entries of the recurrent state concentrate in columns.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#2E5AA8;">4.2  Channel Identity of the Delta Value</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At each token, the recurrence adds the outer product <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{t}u_{t}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{t}u_{t}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> to the decayed state (Eq. <a href="#S2.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 2.1 Gated Delta Rule ‣ 2 Background and Related Work ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). A value channel that
is large across many tokens therefore accumulates in one column, and a key coordinate that is large
across many tokens accumulates in one row. Either factor could therefore produce the columns of Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Column Structure of the State ‣ 4 Channel Structure of the Recurrent State ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>. To separate them, we recompute the state from the recurrence inputs with the channels of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> permuted at
every token, which keeps the values of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> but assigns them to a different channel at each token, and separately
with the entries of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> permuted. With <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> permuted, key-axis groups have the larger max/RMS ratio on all four backbones, and with <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> permuted the difference between the two axes widens (Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2.2 Chunk-wise Kernels ‣ 2 Background and Related Work ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>c).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Finding 3.</span> The columns of large entries in the state come from channels of the delta
value <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> that stay large across tokens.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_section" style="--ltx-fg-color:#2E5AA8;">5  Method</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">PACE has four components (Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>): periodic rounding \scriptsizeA⃝ with a
decode buffer \scriptsizeB⃝ (Section <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Periodic Rounding with a Decode Buffer (\scriptsizeA⃝\scriptsizeB⃝) ‣ 5 Method ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>), which follow from Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Rounding Period and Error Propagation ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>;
channel-aligned scale groups \scriptsizeC⃝ (Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 Channel-Aligned Scale Groups (\scriptsizeC⃝) ‣ 5 Method ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>), from Findings 2 and 3; and
head-level precision allocation \scriptsizeD⃝ (Section <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3 Head-Level Precision Allocation (\scriptsizeD⃝) ‣ 5 Method ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>), from Finding 1. PACE-4 uses
\scriptsizeA⃝–\scriptsizeC⃝; PACE-4/8 adds \scriptsizeD⃝.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#2E5AA8;">5.1  Periodic Rounding with a Decode Buffer (\scriptsizeA⃝\scriptsizeB⃝)</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Rounding every <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dP" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dP</annotation></semantics></math> tokens requires holding the tokens generated since the last rounding in the
decode buffer, which adds to the memory of each configuration. Prior work reports only the stored
state, that is, the <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>-bit payload and its group scales <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>; with one fp16 scale
per <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> values, this gives <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b+16/g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b+16/g</annotation></semantics></math> bits per value. We define <em id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">resident bits per value</em> as the
bits of the stored state and the buffer together, divided by the number of state values. The buffer holds
<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{t},k_{t},v_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{t},k_{t},v_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> per token, the key once per key head and the value once per value head, so with keys
at <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and values at <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> bits it adds per state value</p>
<table id="S5.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\,dP,\qquad c=\frac{b_{k}\,d_{k}/\gamma+b_{v}\,d_{v}+32}{d_{k}d_{v}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\,dP,\qquad c=\frac{b_{k}\,d_{k}/\gamma+b_{v}\,d_{v}+32}{d_{k}d_{v}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of value heads sharing a key head. At <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dP=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dP=1</annotation></semantics></math> the buffer holds only the current token. Resident bits in Figure <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Scale groups. ‣ 5.2 Channel-Aligned Scale Groups (\scriptsizeC⃝) ‣ 5 Method ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Per-Benchmark Scores across Widths and Periods ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> include the buffer;
Table <a href="#A6.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ Appendix F Throughput Measurement and Memory ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> does not. In uniform configurations, which hold every head at the same bit width, the buffer holds keys and
values at eight bits. In the mixed configuration of PACE-4/8, which holds a quarter of the heads at
eight bits, it holds the keys at four bits and the values in bf16, since
rounding the buffered values below eight bits reduces accuracy more than rounding the keys
(Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Separate Key and Value Buffer Widths ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#2E5AA8;">5.2  Channel-Aligned Scale Groups (\scriptsizeC⃝)</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We store the state at four bits in channel-aligned scale groups and keep the per-token decay and the
group scales at higher precision.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Scale groups.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We divide each head’s state <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{k}\times d_{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{k}\times d_{v}}</annotation></semantics></math> into groups of <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> consecutive entries
along the key axis, so that each group holds <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> entries of one column, and store one fp16 scale per
group (Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, \scriptsizeC⃝). Uniform configurations round symmetrically with a step size proportional to the absolute maximum of the group at <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b+16/g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b+16/g</annotation></semantics></math> bits per value; the mixed configuration uses an affine
grid per group whose zero point is stored as a <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>-bit code, at <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b+(16+b)/32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b+(16+b)/32</annotation></semantics></math> bits per value (Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Integer Zero Point ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>). Prior work groups along the value axis
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>. We call our groups channel-aligned because each takes its entries from one
channel of the delta value (Finding 3), whereas a group along the value axis spans columns of different
magnitude, and its step size follows the largest of them. We round to nearest throughout.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.29545v1/fig_t1_resident.svg" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/114;" width="476" height="114"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Mean score over GSM8K, IFEval and RULER-32k against resident bits per value for uniform
value-axis states of four to eight bits on the three Qwen3.5 backbones (Qwen3.6-35B-A3B in
Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Per-Benchmark Scores across Widths and Periods ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>). Each line is one width, with <math id="S5.F4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dP=1,8,32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dP=1,8,32</annotation></semantics></math> from left to
right; dotted: full precision; shaded: above the <math id="S5.F4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9.9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9.9</annotation></semantics></math> bits of DAMP INT8. Right: resident bits per value
against <math id="S5.F4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dP" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dP</annotation></semantics></math> (Eq. <a href="#S5.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ 5.1 Periodic Rounding with a Decode Buffer (\scriptsizeA⃝\scriptsizeB⃝) ‣ 5 Method ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>).</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#2E5AA8;">5.3  Head-Level Precision Allocation (\scriptsizeD⃝)</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We hold the heads that retain rounding error longest at eight bits and the rest at four, applying the allocation criterion of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. [33]</a></cite> per head instead of per channel
(Finding 1). For each head <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>, we measure the energy <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> of the error that one rounding adds and
assign eight bits to the heads with the largest retained error <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> (Eq. <a href="#S3.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ 3.2 Error Propagation ‣ 3 Rounding Period and Error Propagation ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). The ranking is computed once per checkpoint on eight
held-out windows of <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2{,}048" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2,048</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2{,}048</annotation></semantics></math> tokens. With a quarter of the heads per layer at <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math> bits, the payload is
<math id="S5.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.75\cdot 4.625+0.25\cdot 8.75=5.66" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>0.75</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>4.625</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>0.25</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>8.75</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>5.66</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.75\cdot 4.625+0.25\cdot 8.75=5.66</annotation></semantics></math> bits per value, the same as DAMP INT4.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate two variants: PACE-4, a uniform four-bit state with symmetric key-axis groups of <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>64</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64</annotation></semantics></math> and
an int8 buffer, and PACE-4/8, which adds head allocation, uses key-axis groups of <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>32</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32</annotation></semantics></math> with an integer
zero point (Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Integer Zero Point ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>), and holds the buffered keys at four bits and the values in bf16.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_section" style="--ltx-fg-color:#2E5AA8;">6  Experimental Setup</h2>

<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Models.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate Qwen3.5-4B, 9B and 27B and Qwen3.6-35B-A3B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, hybrids that
interleave gated-delta layers with full-attention layers. The gated-delta layers run on the <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">fla</span> kernels.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Benchmarks.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate on GSM8K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, MMLU-Pro <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> on a subset of <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>40</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40</annotation></semantics></math> questions per subject (<math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n{=}560" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>560</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n{=}560</annotation></semantics></math>), IFEval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> (<math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n{=}541" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>541</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n{=}541</annotation></semantics></math>), RULER variable tracking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> at <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32{,}768" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>32,768</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32{,}768</annotation></semantics></math> tokens, and MATH-500
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>. Perplexity is measured on WikiText-103 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>.
All generative tasks
are zero-shot with the chat template and thinking disabled.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Baselines.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare against the full-precision state, against DAMP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> and against the
decoupled scale quantization (DSQ) of Q-Mamba <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, which we reimplement. DAMP INT8 follows the published
configuration: value-axis groups of <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>32</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32</annotation></semantics></math> at eight bits, and the <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>128</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">128</annotation></semantics></math> key channels per head with the
highest error–persistence score held in fp16. It rounds the state after every generated token and
serves as our reference for resident memory; we list it at its published <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9.9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9.9</annotation></semantics></math> bits per value. DAMP INT4 applies the same construction at a four-bit base, with <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>32</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32</annotation></semantics></math> key channels per head held
at eight bits. Q-Mamba DSQ scales the state by one fp16 vector per axis, computed at each rounding; we
implement the quantizer without the fine-tuning step of the published method.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Protocol.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All benchmarks run in the LM Evaluation Harness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> with greedy decoding. Generation
is capped at <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1{,}024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1,024</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1{,}024</annotation></semantics></math> tokens for GSM8K and MATH-500 and <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8{,}192" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8,192</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8{,}192</annotation></semantics></math> for MMLU-Pro. We report GSM8K (flexible-extract filter) and MATH-500 as exact match, IFEval as prompt-level strict
accuracy, and RULER as variable-tracking accuracy. Unless stated otherwise, every quantized configuration uses a four-bit base
width and an int8 decode buffer at <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dP{=}8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dP{=}8</annotation></semantics></math>. Tables <a href="#S7.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 7.2 Main Results ‣ 7 Results ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#S7.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 7.3 Ablations ‣ 7 Results ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Per-Benchmark Scores across Widths and Periods ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> come from separate evaluation runs, each with its own full-precision row.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_section" style="--ltx-fg-color:#2E5AA8;">7  Results</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first isolate the decode period (Section <a href="#S7.SS1" title="7.1 Effect of the Decode Period ‣ 7 Results ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1</span></a>), then compare PACE with DAMP and
Q-Mamba (Section <a href="#S7.SS2" title="7.2 Main Results ‣ 7 Results ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.2</span></a>), test its
components one at a time (Section <a href="#S7.SS3" title="7.3 Ablations ‣ 7 Results ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.3</span></a>), and measure state drift
(Section <a href="#S7.SS4" title="7.4 State Drift under Periodic Rounding ‣ 7 Results ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.4</span></a>) and decoding throughput (Section <a href="#S7.SS5" title="7.5 Throughput ‣ 7 Results ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#2E5AA8;">7.1  Effect of the Decode Period</h3>

<div id="S7.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To isolate the period, we use the quantizer of prior work, uniform round-to-nearest with value-axis
groups of <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>32</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32</annotation></semantics></math>, at four to eight bits and <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dP\in\{1,8,32\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dP\in\{1,8,32\}</annotation></semantics></math> (Figure <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Scale groups. ‣ 5.2 Channel-Aligned Scale Groups (\scriptsizeC⃝) ‣ 5 Method ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>; per-benchmark scores in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Per-Benchmark Scores across Widths and Periods ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>). Per-token rounding loses more accuracy as the width falls, and rounding every eight tokens scores above
per-token rounding at every width below eight bits on all four backbones.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The default period of eight closes most of the gap that a period of <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>32</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32</annotation></semantics></math> closes, with a quarter of
the buffer memory, and matches the flush interval of DeltaLog <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. On the three larger backbones a gap to full
precision remains at every width, concentrated in IFEval and RULER-32k. The RULER-32k gap is similar at all three decode periods, which suggests that this part of the gap arises in prefill.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#2E5AA8;">7.2  Main Results</h3>

<div id="S7.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S7.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 7.2 Main Results ‣ 7 Results ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> compares PACE with DAMP and Q-Mamba DSQ on four backbones. On the three larger backbones PACE-4/8 scores below DAMP INT8 on
IFEval and close to it on average, and the other benchmarks differ in both directions. On Qwen3.5-4B its
average is lower, with lower scores on GSM8K, MMLU-Pro and IFEval.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.T1" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S7.T1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:288.1pt;vertical-align:-142.4pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-65.3pt,54.5pt) scale(0.7,0.7) ;">
