<section id="S7.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#2E5AA8;">7.4  State Drift under Periodic Rounding</h3>

<div id="S7.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To measure the effect of the rounding period on the state itself, we compare the quantized state with
the full-precision state at the same token. We run Qwen3.5-4B on the first 512 tokens of the
WikiText-103 test set <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, round the state every <math id="S7.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dP" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dP</annotation></semantics></math> tokens over the whole sequence, for <math id="S7.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dP" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dP</annotation></semantics></math> from 1 to 64, and report the median over recurrent layers of the relative error <math id="S7.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|S_{q}-S\|_{F}/\|S\|_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|S_{q}-S\|_{F}/\|S\|_{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, which
we call drift. We compare three four-bit
quantizers: uniform INT4 with value-axis groups, DAMP INT4, and the state
quantizer of PACE-4/8 (Figure <a href="#S7.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 7.4 State Drift under Periodic Rounding ‣ 7 Results ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.29545v1/fig_drift.svg" id="S7.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/201;" width="476" height="201"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Relative state error against token position on Qwen3.5-4B, one curve per period <math id="S7.F5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dP" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dP</annotation></semantics></math>. The DAMP panel (right) keeps <math id="S7.F5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16</annotation></semantics></math>
key channels per head in fp16.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S7.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For all three quantizers, drift decreases as <math id="S7.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dP" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dP</annotation></semantics></math> grows at every token position, and the
same ordering holds on Qwen3.6-35B-A3B (Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B State Drift ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>). Drift grows more
slowly than the number of roundings, which is consistent with a decaying recurrence that removes old
rounding error while each rounding adds new error (Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Error Propagation ‣ 3 Rounding Period and Error Propagation ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#2E5AA8;">7.5  Throughput</h3>

<div id="S7.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We measure decoding cost on Qwen3.5-4B on one H100 PCIe GPU at a <math id="S7.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>512</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">512</annotation></semantics></math>-token context and batch sizes
from <math id="S7.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S7.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>128</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">128</annotation></semantics></math>. We compare per-token decoding with a bf16 state, the decode buffer with a bf16 state
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>, and periodic decoding at <math id="S7.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dP{=}8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dP{=}8</annotation></semantics></math> with a uniform INT4 state and an int8
buffer, for which we implement Triton kernels that apply the buffered tokens to the packed state. We
report time per output token (TPOT) and the latency of the state update (Figure <a href="#S7.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 7.5 Throughput ‣ 7 Results ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, Appendix <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Throughput Measurement and Memory ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Periodic rounding lets a four-bit state keep the speedup of the decode buffer. Periodic INT4 decoding
has a lower TPOT than per-token bf16 decoding from batch 16 upward, and the difference grows with batch
size, as reading and writing the per-sequence state take a growing share of each decoding step. The decode
buffer with a bf16 state accounts for most of this reduction, and the INT4 state reduces TPOT slightly
further.
The memory each configuration holds per sequence, measured in the same run, agrees with the resident bits of Eq. <a href="#S5.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ 5.1 Periodic Rounding with a Decode Buffer (\scriptsizeA⃝\scriptsizeB⃝) ‣ 5 Method ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> (Appendix <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Throughput Measurement and Memory ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.29545v1/fig_throughput.svg" id="S7.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/157;" width="381" height="157"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>Decoding cost on Qwen3.5-4B (H100 PCIe, 512-token context) against batch size. Left:
latency of the recurrent-state update. Right: time per output token. The shading marks the gap to bf16. Labels give the ratio to bf16 (left) and the relative change in
TPOT (right) at the ends of each curve.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_section" style="--ltx-fg-color:#2E5AA8;">8  Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduced PACE, a post-hoc four-bit state quantizer for gated-delta models that rounds the state
once every few generated tokens and holds the tokens in between in a decode buffer. For uniform
configurations, a period of eight tokens reduces the accuracy loss at every width below eight bits on
four backbones. This is consistent with the bound of Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Rounding Period and Error Propagation ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, under which more decay
between two roundings leaves less of each rounding error in the state. The large entries of the state
gather in columns that follow the channels of the delta value, and PACE forms each scale group from
the entries of one such column. PACE-4/8 comes close to the accuracy of DAMP INT8 on the three larger
backbones at about two thirds of its resident memory, and PACE-4 scores higher than Q-Mamba DSQ at
equal resident bits. Periodic rounding also keeps the decoding speedup of the decode buffer.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Limitations</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate four hybrid models from the Qwen3.5 and Qwen3.6 families, all of which use the gated delta rule.
Periodic rounding applies to any linear-attention layer whose state can be advanced over several tokens
at once, which includes the chunked forms of gated linear attention, Mamba-2 and DeltaNet variants.
The head decays that enter the error bound, the column structure that motivates key-axis scale groups,
and the head ranking are measured only on these models. None of them is shown to carry over to
other model families, to models whose layers are all recurrent, or to token mixers with a different
update or decay.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Periodic rounding changes when the state is rounded and leaves the choice of quantizer open. Besides PACE, we evaluate DAMP INT4 and Q-Mamba DSQ under the same period. We do not test quantizers
with static scales, stochastic rounding or rotations of the state under periodic rounding.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The configuration space of periodic state quantization is large. It spans the prefill and decode
periods, the bit widths of the state and of the buffered keys and values, the axis and width of the
scale groups, and the fraction of heads held at eight bits, and we explore only part of it. The
prefill period in particular is fixed at the <math id="Sx1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>256</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256</annotation></semantics></math>-token chunk of the kernel in all benchmark evaluations, so
the effect of rounding the state more or less often during prefill is not measured.
Throughput is timed only for a uniform four-bit state; the key-axis groups and head allocation of PACE
are not timed.</p>
</div>
</section>
