<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Separate Key and Value Buffer Widths</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To locate which buffered tensor limits the buffer width, we round only the keys or only the values to
four bits and keep the other in bf16, with the state quantizer of PACE-4/8 at <math id="A4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dP{=}8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dP{=}8</annotation></semantics></math>. Rounding the
values reduces accuracy more than rounding the keys (Table <a href="#A4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Appendix D Separate Key and Value Buffer Widths ‣ PACE: Periodic and Channel-Aligned Encodingof Four-Bit Recurrent States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). The rank-one form of the
update predicts the opposite order, since a key error misaddresses both the read and the write, while a
value error affects only the write. Rounding either tensor to four
bits gives a similar relative input error, but the kernel L2-normalizes the buffered keys after
rounding, so only the direction of a key error reaches the state, while a value error enters it
unchanged.</p>
</div>
